rachael: Customer escalation — streaming SSE from TS SDK drops final small chunks when AbortController is used. Also seeing duplicate billing events when client reconnects quickly. Customer: FinServe, req id x-req-id=fin-abc123, started 2024-03-10T16:22Z. Repro attached.
kevin: Can you paste the repro? Is this using EventSource polyfill or our fetch-based SSE helper?
rachael: Minimal repro (TS, Node fetch streaming):
```
const ac = new AbortController();
const res = await fetch(url, { signal: ac.signal });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
try {
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    processChunk(value);
  }
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === 'AbortError') console.log('aborted');
  throw err;
}
// customer calls ac.abort() from UI action while awaiting reader.read()
```
When abort happens while awaiting read(), the client often never sees the final ~10-20 byte chunk. Server log shows final chunk emitted. Billing: when client reconnects fast we get duplicate token accounting traces.
oliver: Looks like the earlier fix for Node stream cancellation introduced a race: abort path marks stream consumed before flushing pending chunk buffers for the TS SDK (v2.7.1). Can you confirm SDK version?
rachael: Confirmed v2.7.1. Customer repro with a tiny server that emits final chunk then closes — curl sees it; TS client misses it ~40% of runs. Trace id fin-abc123.
eng-runtime-oncall: Reproducer helpful. Short-term: adding micro delay before abort helps, but obviously not acceptable for customer. Reassigning to jack in runtime to dig deeper. Also check auth rotation: customer rotates tokens every 10s.
jack: Pulled trace. When reader.read() rejects with AbortError we mark stream as "consumed" and teardown. Underlying socket sometimes still has bytes pending in buffer. Our reconnect logic thinks request didn't complete and retries, causing duplicate billing. Repro steps look straightforward — I'll run locally with --inspect.
kevin: FYI customer tried Python sdk async iterator: they don't see duplicate billing there but do see the iterator hang when abort called from a callback; different semantics across SDKs.
jack: Good note. Hypothesis: JS/TS SDK calls controller.abort() and closes before drain; Python's async iterator returns differently. Quick patch idea: on abort path, call flushPendingBuffers() (await it) before marking consumed=true and before emitting final 'end' to app. That preserves last chunk and avoids premature reconnect.
rachael: Customer accepted temporary workaround: delay abort by 50ms and handle final done marker explicitly:
```
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  if (chunk === null) break;
  // process
}
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 50);
```
but they want a proper fix.
support-bot: Incident created: INC-2024-0421. Linked PR draft: rtx/ts-sdk#789. Hotfix ETA: 48h.
oliver: Also need unit tests for concurrent abort + auth rotation; CI uncovered a flaky case recently where token refresh triggered mid-stream.
jack: I'll push a patch to 2.7.x branch with test that simulates read() rejecting while socket has bytes. Will include metrics suppression so we don't emit duplicate billing events when stream aborts cleanly. Assigning QA to lily.
lily: I'll run verification matrix across Node 18/20 and Electron 19. Any logs/flags to enable for debug runs?
jack: Use REDWOOD_STREAM_DEBUG=1 and include the x-req-id header in logs so we can correlate to customer traces.
rachael: Thanks — I'll update the customer with workaround, incident link, and ETA. I'll attach the customer's trace and the minimal server repro to the incident.
kevin: One more note from customer: they see the issue more often when final chunk size < 32 bytes; maybe buffer threshold interaction.
jack: Good catch — we'll add focused test on small final-chunk sizes and also test concurrent token refresh during that window.
lily: I'll post QA results here once complete.
rachael: Appreciate the quick follow-up. Will close loop with FinServe after patch verification.
